01:21:58.500 --> 01:22:00.840
Probably very fast for you guys, but we took a very nice break.

01:22:00.860 --> 01:22:01.780
I had a glass of water.

01:22:02.060 --> 01:22:03.300
It was two weeks ago that we last...

01:22:04.060 --> 01:22:06.920
It's been two weeks, and I have prepared the developer interviews.

01:22:06.920 --> 01:22:11.500
All of them with Miyamoto, because Tezuka really didn't have much to say.

01:22:12.680 --> 01:22:22.440
So with that in mind, the first interview comes from around the time that they were finishing up development of the Super Famicom version of Link to the Past.

01:22:22.880 --> 01:22:28.080
And so there was some reflection stuff on, like, what would you have wanted to do differently and stuff like that.

01:22:28.120 --> 01:22:36.300
So the question, to be exact, was when you first started the development of the Super Famicom Zelda, did you have a specific vision in mind?

01:22:36.300 --> 01:22:38.880
Like, you wanted the game to be a certain way?

01:22:39.480 --> 01:22:42.420
Miyamoto. Zelda 1 had an inadequate system.

01:22:42.620 --> 01:22:46.560
I wanted to do things that weren't capable of doing in the first game.

01:22:46.880 --> 01:22:51.060
To call the first Zelda inadequate seems very harsh to me, the interviewer notes.

01:22:51.560 --> 01:22:52.740
Miyamoto comes back with,

01:22:52.740 --> 01:22:57.420
There were a lot of things we intended to do, but we kept getting blocked by hardware constraints.

01:22:57.740 --> 01:22:59.860
So the interviewer presses for examples.

01:23:00.760 --> 01:23:10.720
Miyamoto answers, For the level 7 dungeon entrance, we changed the color of the ground when the water drained, but we had originally intended to have the water actually disappear.

01:23:11.180 --> 01:23:15.980
And you can burn small trees, but we intended for you to be able to burn down big ones.

01:23:16.300 --> 01:23:22.580
There were a lot of little things like that, and I wanted the Super Famicom Zelda to be more realistic in that regard.

01:23:22.580 --> 01:23:24.080
In addition,

01:23:24.680 --> 01:23:26.140
Wait for it, wait for it.

01:23:26.340 --> 01:23:36.500
In addition, back when Legend of Zelda was being made, having a world based on swords and magic was still a fresh idea, as was the concept of being able to save your game.

01:23:36.720 --> 01:23:42.380
A system that allowed you to buy in-game items was also new, not to mention solving dungeons.

01:23:43.160 --> 01:23:44.560
Also, I'm joking.

01:23:44.560 --> 01:23:51.480
However, in the five years since the game's release, a lot of titles have appeared on the market that do that same sort of thing.

01:23:51.680 --> 01:23:53.800
So the sense of innovation has disappeared.

01:23:54.000 --> 01:23:57.360
I thought hard about what we could do next that would entertain the players.

01:23:57.720 --> 01:24:03.980
On the other hand, we weren't just about to cut out shopping and dungeons entirely, just because they were no longer innovative.

01:24:04.480 --> 01:24:10.800
Alright. My first, my first just like, basic question of this is, what is he talking about with the, the water disappearing?

01:24:10.960 --> 01:24:11.800
Doesn't the water disappear?

01:24:12.240 --> 01:24:13.600
Well, it turns into sand.

01:24:13.600 --> 01:24:14.740
The animation. Yeah.

01:24:15.740 --> 01:24:17.200
And then second of all, first of all.

01:24:17.200 --> 01:24:19.200
What would be the use of burning down big trees?

01:24:19.340 --> 01:24:22.200
Right. A lot of those things are so specific and seem unnecessary.

01:24:22.220 --> 01:24:24.000
Things that he got stuck on, though, during development.

01:24:24.220 --> 01:24:25.860
You know, it's like, to us it means nothing.

01:24:26.040 --> 01:24:29.640
Right. But to him, he had another big plan ready to move.

01:24:29.640 --> 01:24:31.240
I'm sure that was like hours of work.

01:24:31.260 --> 01:24:33.220
It's like, why won't the big tree burn?

01:24:34.980 --> 01:24:36.420
Like, we can't pull it off.

01:24:36.820 --> 01:24:42.160
Yeah. And he probably wanted it to burn like, it would start off small and then get grand and bigger.

01:24:42.360 --> 01:24:43.460
Scissors are floating up into the air.

01:24:43.600 --> 01:24:45.740
They wanted like wildfire physics going on.

01:24:45.880 --> 01:24:47.240
Well, I mean, they do have that later.

01:24:47.380 --> 01:24:57.660
It's funny that you bring up the physics thing, though, too, because like in the Breath of the Wild, like the prototype build, they did make Legend of Zelda 1, like, but with Breath of the Wild physics.

01:24:57.980 --> 01:25:01.120
Wow. Just to see like how that stuff would work.

01:25:01.320 --> 01:25:06.660
Like, you know, like what if you put a tree on fire and then it spread, you know, like how it would.

01:25:06.960 --> 01:25:13.180
So that's how they develop like what core concepts they wanted in Breath of the Wild by going back to the original Legend of Zelda.

01:25:13.580 --> 01:25:16.000
And this prototype build will someday get out there.

01:25:16.060 --> 01:25:18.000
I can only hope. Kind of wish they included it.

01:25:18.100 --> 01:25:22.540
Like, I wish an area in Breath of the Wild was just the entire game of the original Legend of Zelda.

01:25:22.540 --> 01:25:27.660
Yeah, that would be I mean, like in many ways there are like homages to the original Legend of Zelda.

01:25:27.660 --> 01:25:42.620
And that even includes actually like I guess, you know, this is a stretch for me, but there is a small little island in the far northeast in Breath of the Wild that is very much like the island that you would take like your raft to get there.

01:25:42.720 --> 01:25:46.700
Like it's like a square with like instead of being there's a labyrinth there now.

01:25:46.840 --> 01:25:49.460
But like, you know, I like little nods like that.

01:25:49.580 --> 01:25:51.400
That's cool. Do you guys want another quote?

01:25:51.980 --> 01:25:54.520
Yes. Hit me. OK.

01:25:54.980 --> 01:25:55.940
Play it again, Sam.

01:25:55.940 --> 01:26:01.440
This is Miyamoto on just like, you know, how he approaches game design.

01:26:01.560 --> 01:26:09.360
OK, I don't want to make games where the player is just a puppet in the hands of the creator playing exactly as scripted.

01:26:09.460 --> 01:26:15.440
Trying to get players to become better and better at your game is certainly one valid approach to making games.

01:26:15.620 --> 01:26:24.740
But for me, I want to present games to players that are more like pure toys, something you use, explore and play with freely.

01:26:24.740 --> 01:26:33.200
And then the interviewer adds the Legend of Zelda took the RPG genre from a place of relative obscurity and made it friendly and easy to understand.

01:26:33.520 --> 01:26:36.060
In that sense, it was a huge success for the gaming world.

01:26:36.240 --> 01:26:37.500
What are your thoughts on that?

01:26:37.780 --> 01:26:39.120
Miyamoto says, thank you.

01:26:40.460 --> 01:26:47.080
For that one, I wanted to create a game world that conveyed the same feeling you get when you're exploring a new city for the first time.

01:26:47.280 --> 01:26:55.420
How fun would it be, I thought, if I could make the player identify with the main character in the game and get completely lost and immersed in that world?

01:26:55.420 --> 01:27:00.360
The idea of a game in real time also was something I'd been thinking about for a while.

01:27:00.620 --> 01:27:05.940
But with such limited memory, it turned out to be a huge challenge to create enough satisfying content for players.

01:27:06.200 --> 01:27:10.380
I absolutely wanted to avoid half-baked ideas and gameplay.

01:27:10.780 --> 01:27:18.920
Since Zelda was released, I think the market has seen an increase in mean-spirited, petty games, and I still feel that sense of responsibility.

01:27:19.160 --> 01:27:19.960
I have a couple questions.

01:27:20.240 --> 01:27:24.180
When he says play in real time, did he want like a day-night cycle or something?

01:27:24.180 --> 01:27:25.340
That would have been neat, right?

01:27:25.640 --> 01:27:30.920
I think he just means more like the idea that you're living in a breathable world.

01:27:31.260 --> 01:27:32.920
Instead of like...

01:27:32.920 --> 01:27:34.180
Like some sort of breath.

01:27:34.460 --> 01:27:36.980
Right, right. Like a wild breath.

01:27:37.480 --> 01:27:38.740
Like a breath of the wild.

01:27:38.880 --> 01:27:47.060
No, more like, you know how level 1-1 of Super Mario Bros., that's just as much of a world as Legend of Zelda,

01:27:47.140 --> 01:27:50.420
except for in that one, things are scripted and they just happen.

01:27:50.580 --> 01:27:52.840
I think he was looking more for like...

01:27:52.840 --> 01:27:54.020
I think he's happy.

01:27:54.180 --> 01:27:57.640
With the real-time world he created in the original Legend of Zelda.

01:27:58.120 --> 01:28:02.200
But I'm sure he would have also, if the ability to have a day-and-night thing would have happened,

01:28:02.340 --> 01:28:04.120
like he would have loved to do that.

01:28:04.280 --> 01:28:06.900
He probably would have loved to also have like the times change.

01:28:08.240 --> 01:28:11.740
I would interpret that also as like, you kill an enemy, it stays dead.

01:28:12.340 --> 01:28:14.300
Yeah, that would have been interesting to see.

01:28:14.380 --> 01:28:16.200
And like it just also is like laying there.

01:28:16.580 --> 01:28:19.000
Oh, no, no, no. I mean, that's what happens though in the game.

01:28:19.360 --> 01:28:21.120
You know? Well, if you're in a couple screens, I think.

01:28:21.120 --> 01:28:27.200
If you're in a dungeon, I mean, you know, you're in a dungeon, you kill an enemy, and you clear a room, there are no more enemies in that room.

01:28:27.380 --> 01:28:29.800
Yeah, yeah, yeah. I meant that it would stay there dead.

01:28:30.220 --> 01:28:31.240
Like if we could see it.

01:28:31.260 --> 01:28:32.280
Yeah, because of course. That's a little bit.

01:28:32.300 --> 01:28:33.180
It's a little bit much to me.

01:28:33.200 --> 01:28:34.080
Real time, it would rot.

01:28:34.180 --> 01:28:36.160
And what does he mean? That's that mean-spirited petty game.

01:28:36.360 --> 01:28:38.540
I was going to say, what does he mean by mean-spirited and petty?

01:28:38.640 --> 01:28:40.160
Just like more violent games?

01:28:40.240 --> 01:28:46.680
Yeah. And especially because this interview that he's done takes place in like the late 90s.

01:28:46.860 --> 01:28:50.360
So by then we're seeing like a rise in FPSs and stuff like that too.

01:28:50.480 --> 01:28:55.320
And I think he's just more like, hey, like, you know, can we go back to making video games fun?

01:28:55.480 --> 01:28:56.980
And it's like, Miyamoto, you're old.

01:28:59.200 --> 01:29:00.020
Joe, did you have a thought?

01:29:00.020 --> 01:29:08.980
Well, I was just thinking that if when I heard the mean-spirited petty thing, I thought he meant more like, I guess this is more games around that time period that Zelda came out.

01:29:09.140 --> 01:29:18.660
But more like gameplay mechanics that are kind of cheap or you beat a game and it says, yeah, but do it all again before you really beat the game, even though that happened before.

01:29:18.920 --> 01:29:20.180
But later, that's the same day.

01:29:20.920 --> 01:29:22.120
Yeah, but later, that's the same day.

01:29:22.400 --> 01:29:26.040
Considering the Zelda series as a whole, what themes are you trying to convey?

01:29:26.040 --> 01:29:30.180
The cover art for the Famicom Legend of Zelda emphasized the feeling of exploration.

01:29:30.300 --> 01:29:35.380
And I picked this one because I actually think Miyamoto has something to say this time around.

01:29:35.940 --> 01:29:41.980
As an everyday boy gets drawn into a series of incredible events and grows to become a hero.

01:29:42.300 --> 01:29:48.780
Within that framework, I wanted to create a game where the player could experience the feeling of exploration as he travels around the world,

01:29:48.980 --> 01:29:53.000
becoming familiar with the history of the land and the natural world he inhabits.

01:29:53.000 --> 01:29:57.260
That is reflected in the title, The Legend of Blank.

01:29:57.840 --> 01:29:58.820
He includes the blank.

01:29:58.960 --> 01:30:06.480
I don't. But I think that that's like a neat reason for why he would call the series The Legend of Zelda and like that whole framework.

01:30:06.700 --> 01:30:12.620
But then goes on to add, adventure games and RPGs are games where you advance the story through dialogue alone.

01:30:12.780 --> 01:30:20.220
But we wanted players to actually experience the physical sensation of using a controller and moving the character through the world.

01:30:20.220 --> 01:30:24.240
We wanted dungeons to be explorable with a simple mapping system.

01:30:24.540 --> 01:30:28.400
These and similar ideas were what we wanted to experiment with in Zelda.

01:30:28.740 --> 01:30:32.520
These themes are carried forward in the Super Famicom Zelda as well.

01:30:32.880 --> 01:30:34.980
I think he's mis...

01:30:34.980 --> 01:30:39.580
He's not entirely giving an accurate description of what an RPG is.

01:30:39.760 --> 01:30:41.060
Like, I think he's selling it short.

01:30:41.200 --> 01:30:42.080
I think, yeah.

01:30:42.140 --> 01:30:46.640
I think when he's talking about RPG there, he's probably more thinking like just tabletop gaming.

01:30:46.840 --> 01:30:48.980
Just because you're talking about action.

01:30:48.980 --> 01:30:54.540
And this is weird too because now at that interview point, we are in like 93, 94.

01:30:54.960 --> 01:30:58.220
And so like there are like Dragon Quests and Final Fantasy out there.

01:30:58.360 --> 01:31:00.780
So there is like another way to do RPGs.

01:31:00.940 --> 01:31:12.380
But I, you know, I still, I like his like justification too for like how like gaming is like a different form of role playing than, you know, than tabletop gaming.

01:31:12.580 --> 01:31:14.000
And like both are valid ways.

01:31:14.140 --> 01:31:17.060
But here's what we try to do differently in video games.

01:31:27.600 --> 01:31:34.940
And so just very briefly, this one isn't so much about Zelda, but I stumbled upon it on the Legend of Zelda developer interviews for the NES Classic.

01:31:34.940 --> 01:31:44.200
This one is about the NES and what the NES, specifically the NES means to Miyamoto, Tezuka, and Koji Kondo.

01:31:44.360 --> 01:31:48.760
And I thought that was interesting to hear like their thoughts on it because we're always talking about what the NES means to us.

01:31:49.440 --> 01:31:53.940
So Miyamoto describes it as one word, competition.

01:32:07.580 --> 01:32:11.320
Do we think of the NES ever as competition?

01:32:12.100 --> 01:32:12.960
Not really.

01:32:13.140 --> 01:32:15.620
To me, it's just like what existed.

01:32:16.120 --> 01:32:19.520
Right. I think like in a way it almost like it dominated.

01:32:19.920 --> 01:32:21.380
Yeah. I think of it as more like domination.

01:32:21.380 --> 01:32:29.140
Yeah, yeah. Are they talking about like economic competition or are we talking about like multiplayer games playing against each other competition?

01:32:29.420 --> 01:32:32.240
He's talking more like competition in the first sense.

01:32:32.400 --> 01:32:42.760
Like the fact that like the competition between like games that you could make on the NES versus games you could make on a computer and games you could make on the Sega Master System.

01:32:42.760 --> 01:32:55.740
Okay. You know, and like that the NES was like now entering the foray and was going to like establish a name for Nintendo as like a video game console maker, not just a heart, not just an arcade maker.

01:32:56.420 --> 01:33:02.860
I think another part where for Kondo he goes, it's kind of a basis.

01:33:03.160 --> 01:33:06.720
I've always worked in video game music and I think it's thanks to the NES.

01:33:07.320 --> 01:33:14.600
So for him, in the days of the NES, I learned how to compose video game music and about the importance of sound effects.

01:33:14.900 --> 01:33:20.680
Then along with the development of hardware, the sounds improved in quality and we were able to use a greater variety of sounds.

01:33:20.820 --> 01:33:25.640
But when it comes to the essence of composing game music, the crucial elements haven't changed at all.

01:33:25.820 --> 01:33:30.340
In that respect as well, I owe it to the NES that I'm able to write all kinds of music today.

01:33:30.900 --> 01:33:35.060
Really interesting to think that he didn't have much of a music background before the NES.

01:33:35.600 --> 01:33:40.160
Right. It's a very unique path in the musical career path.

01:33:40.320 --> 01:33:47.280
I do think that having those limitations, you know, causes a lot of creativity though, especially when you're writing music.

01:33:47.460 --> 01:33:52.800
Like when I'm doing the chiptune stuff, you, I mean, you have, you only have four tracks to do it with.

01:33:52.880 --> 01:33:58.860
So you have to be really specific with what you want to do and it forces you to think in a certain way.

01:33:58.860 --> 01:34:04.660
And when you have to think in a certain way, you kind of find like ways around obstacles that you wouldn't normally think about.

01:34:04.820 --> 01:34:14.900
It makes sense why he's such a good video game music composer and why someone who like, and not saying that like normal musicians can't come on and, you know, try and score video games.

01:34:15.040 --> 01:34:16.100
Because they have. Right.

01:34:16.220 --> 01:34:18.000
They definitely have. And then some of them are great.

01:34:18.000 --> 01:34:35.200
But I think like, you know, when you, when your whole life has been around just designing video game soundtracks, you come up with these, you know, weird quirks and ways of thinking about music to Sam's point that you wouldn't have thought about if you were coming from like a more like, you know, a rock and roll background or a classical background.

01:34:35.200 --> 01:34:36.320
So that is interesting.

01:34:36.720 --> 01:34:49.160
Even like, like a film, even if you're scoring films, I mean, imagine like you got, you got to think about in video game, you got to think about things being loopable or like making, you know, making sense in an action environment and not, you know, not kind of overtaking.

01:34:49.160 --> 01:34:52.920
Right. Led Zeppelin wasn't worried about how their song would sound over and over and over again.

01:34:53.680 --> 01:34:58.800
And then last but not least, I think Tezuka sums it up best how most of our listeners probably feel.

01:34:59.140 --> 01:35:02.160
Tezuka thinks of it as a magic box.

01:35:02.420 --> 01:35:06.380
It's a single device, but by changing cartridge, you can play something different.

01:35:06.840 --> 01:35:08.880
Different software provides different experiences.

01:35:09.300 --> 01:35:13.860
It's so small, but the NES Classic Edition is another magic box.

01:35:14.540 --> 01:35:15.340
I mean, yeah.

01:35:15.400 --> 01:35:17.180
Tezuka with the promo language there.

01:35:17.500 --> 01:35:18.780
Yeah, that's what I kind of got at the end.

01:35:20.080 --> 01:35:30.520
And I will link this whole article and interview into the show notes because Miyamoto then goes on a long rant about baseball on NES.

01:35:30.940 --> 01:35:40.940
Quanted. As if, which is, but he goes into this long rant about it and how proud he is of it and like his sense of responsibility for it.

01:35:41.020 --> 01:35:43.940
And I'm like, okay, dude, now it's 2017.

01:35:43.940 --> 01:35:48.200
We really should not be looking back fondly at NES baseball anymore.

01:35:48.880 --> 01:35:54.120
But Legend of Zelda does get a direct sequel for the Nintendo Entertainment System.

01:35:54.320 --> 01:35:56.700
Legend of Zelda 2 Adventure of Link.

01:35:57.360 --> 01:36:01.300
Yeah. And it's completely different than Legend of Zelda 1.

01:36:01.300 --> 01:36:06.680
So we will be playing that and I'm sure we'll, you know, we'll all just love that one too.

01:36:06.780 --> 01:36:12.020
Right, guys? I'm actually looking forward to that one almost more because I've heard so, so much about how different it is.

01:36:12.100 --> 01:36:14.420
And I really don't know what to expect.

01:36:14.620 --> 01:36:16.420
I know, I know one, one major element.

01:36:16.420 --> 01:36:20.720
Yeah, yeah, yeah. And obviously like this, this thing's been like so many games after that too.

01:36:20.960 --> 01:36:28.960
There's like, you know, besides like other just mainline games, there's a bunch of different, you know, like there's Hyrule Warriors now, which is a Musou style game.

01:36:29.240 --> 01:36:36.400
But with Legend of Zelda characters, there's Triforce Heroes, which is with three people, you know, three people with 3DS is playing together.

01:36:36.400 --> 01:36:37.600
Like Soul Calibur 2.

01:36:37.900 --> 01:36:39.120
Soul Calibur 2, yes.

01:36:39.260 --> 01:36:40.740
Link shows up in that one too.

01:36:41.020 --> 01:36:43.580
And there's so many different ways that people think about Link now too.

01:36:44.060 --> 01:36:51.380
Like I think of Link as the Soul Calibur 2 look, you know, like that adult Link that we see in like even the Super Smash Brothers games.

01:36:51.380 --> 01:37:00.160
But like other people think of him as like the cel-shaded Wind Waker and other people think of him as like this young little boy in Legend of Zelda and Link to the Past.

01:37:00.440 --> 01:37:06.380
Or like Big Burly Link from Legend of Zelda Future 9.

01:37:07.400 --> 01:37:09.420
All right, I'll go for a spinoff.

01:37:09.540 --> 01:37:13.100
I want to see one that is linear with branching story paths.

01:37:13.720 --> 01:37:15.120
Oof. We haven't gotten that.

01:37:15.120 --> 01:37:17.040
I don't want to see that at all. That's interesting, but yeah.

01:37:17.360 --> 01:37:18.620
I know people disagree with that.

01:37:18.620 --> 01:37:20.320
Like Telltale's Legend of Zelda?

01:37:20.960 --> 01:37:22.240
No, no, not like that.

01:37:22.440 --> 01:37:27.000
R.I.P. Like decisions that you make to decide what dungeon you go to or something.

01:37:27.140 --> 01:37:29.260
Okay. You know? And not like...

01:37:29.260 --> 01:37:32.680
But then you're locked out of other things because of that choice?

01:37:32.900 --> 01:37:33.920
Yeah. Okay, interesting.

01:37:34.240 --> 01:37:37.840
But there's enough that it doesn't matter and enough that you can replay it in a different way.

01:37:37.980 --> 01:37:40.420
Yeah, and then at the end it just winds up like Mass Effect 3.

01:37:41.300 --> 01:37:45.520
Yeah. Like all these amazing paths and then at the end it's just like, yeah, but the Crucible.

01:37:46.780 --> 01:37:50.220
Okay, and then The Legend of Zelda also had a TV show.

01:37:51.220 --> 01:37:58.100
Yes, The Legend of Zelda TV show with just like, honestly, we should have a podcast where

01:37:58.100 --> 01:37:59.620
we just talk about the show itself.

01:37:59.840 --> 01:38:00.760
Like that should just be a thing.

01:38:00.880 --> 01:38:03.140
We all just watch the first season and just go over it.

01:38:03.440 --> 01:38:05.400
The whole season? Yeah, the whole season.

