01:40:35.700 --> 01:40:41.320
But I still think that if you had a kid sit down with this game, they would be able to

01:40:41.320 --> 01:40:42.580
figure this stuff out without me.

01:40:42.740 --> 01:40:46.140
If they're playing the SP version, they're still willing to play the game.

01:40:46.580 --> 01:40:48.980
So it's not like an unwillingness to play.

01:40:49.140 --> 01:40:53.700
They're still willing to play the game. I just have this image, and maybe I'm just being a grouchy old man, but I just have this

01:40:53.700 --> 01:40:58.660
image in my head of this kid, and he just starts off in the regular game.

01:40:58.740 --> 01:41:00.220
He's like, where's my sword?

01:41:00.260 --> 01:41:01.660
This game is stupid.

01:41:01.960 --> 01:41:05.500
And then he just smashes the controller, and he goes upstairs.

01:41:05.700 --> 01:41:08.160
Yeah, I imagine one of our listeners was like, hey, that was me.

01:41:08.520 --> 01:41:13.440
Yeah. And then the last thing that I want to talk about, because no one really knows about this,

01:41:14.720 --> 01:41:15.600
Zelda BS.

01:41:16.740 --> 01:41:18.220
Does this ring a bell to anybody?

01:41:18.640 --> 01:41:24.960
Zelda BS is for the Satellaview, which was an add-on for the Japanese Super Famicom, where

01:41:24.960 --> 01:41:30.080
it was basically like the first DLC, where you basically paid a monthly subscription fee,

01:41:30.120 --> 01:41:34.680
and then for an hour, this game would be live.

01:41:34.680 --> 01:41:37.940
You could only play it for that specific hour, because it was transmitting the data during

01:41:37.940 --> 01:41:44.780
that time. But it translated the original Legend of Zelda game, what a lot of people now call the third

01:41:44.780 --> 01:41:46.860
quest, because it is like very...

01:41:46.860 --> 01:41:52.020
There are some small differences between this, the second quest, and the third quest, which

01:41:52.020 --> 01:41:56.280
is what the one that the Satellaview is referring to, where it is the original Legend of Zelda

01:41:56.280 --> 01:42:00.500
game, with the combat and all, and the overworld style, and all the enemies, and the layout's

01:42:00.500 --> 01:42:06.100
the same. But it's done in the Link to the Past style, similar to Super Mario All-Stars, you know,

01:42:06.200 --> 01:42:07.820
like how Super Mario All-Stars remastered those.

01:42:08.040 --> 01:42:13.320
There's this Super Nintendo version that no one can really play, except for me.

01:42:13.320 --> 01:42:15.760
I managed to get a hold of this one.

01:42:16.160 --> 01:42:22.740
You can also buy a reproduction cart of these online, but you're not funding anyone other

01:42:22.740 --> 01:42:24.260
than the guy who makes the repo cart.

01:42:24.260 --> 01:42:26.520
So, questionable if you want to do that.

01:42:26.640 --> 01:42:30.300
And it's also like, it's not fully fleshed out, so I don't know if it's worth playing.

01:42:30.300 --> 01:42:34.400
It's not a Super Mario All-Stars necessarily, but it's neat that they have this, right?

01:42:35.380 --> 01:42:37.440
16-bit original Legend of Zelda?

01:42:37.600 --> 01:42:39.040
It's neat that you have that, Mike.

01:42:39.940 --> 01:42:41.100
Well, I don't own it.

01:42:42.680 --> 01:42:46.700
In fact, nobody can own that game, if you think about it, because the Satellaview, like

01:42:46.700 --> 01:42:49.760
you couldn't even transfer that data to your cartridge.

01:42:49.760 --> 01:42:52.980
You needed to just be online at that specific time.

01:42:53.200 --> 01:42:54.500
Wow. That's interesting.

01:42:54.900 --> 01:42:57.300
It's also, it makes sense that it failed.

01:42:57.800 --> 01:42:59.740
Yeah. That's a Satellaview in general, yeah.

01:43:00.120 --> 01:43:01.900
What does the BS stand for?

01:43:01.980 --> 01:43:06.720
You know, it's like, yeah, I know it sounds like bullshit, but it's actually like a Japanese

01:43:06.720 --> 01:43:10.440
thing. Like, the BS does stand for something, but not something I can tell you.

